-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/40876a02-83f0-42e6-90a1-f12c857bafb6
-- title:
--   Successful rational acceptance never moves the tape cursor backward
-- statement:
--   For a rational ratio $v$, the bounded rational acceptance procedure returns the candidate immediately if $v\ge1$. If $v<1$, it makes a bounded uniform draw from $\{0,\ldots,d-1\}$, where $d$ is the reduced positive denominator of $v$, and chooses the candidate when the draw is less than the absolute numerator; otherwise it keeps the original state. A failed draw returns no result. Let $A$ be any type, $x,z,y\in A$, $\omega:\mathbb N\to\{0,1\}$ a tape, $T\in\mathbb N$ a trial cap, $v\in\mathbb Q$ any ratio, and $c,u\in\mathbb N$. Then $$\operatorname{rationalAccept}_{\omega,T,v}(x,z,c)=\operatorname{some}(y,u)\ \Longrightarrow\ c\le u.$$
-- source:
--   https://github.com/meelgroup/counting-matroid/blob/dd16a4628fdb095c58fe0f3dfd8e353c6e97443c/CountingMatroid/Analysis/ProgramExchangeAcceptanceSubkernel.lean#L53-L69

